From 21559ea300ff2fe60a3bcb3c593db9b1b29692d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:57:18 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] mdadm.8, mdadm.conf.5: document the amd metadata format

Describe --metadata=amd: what it is, the levels it supports (a Volume
needs md-linear), --create and --grow of a RAIDABLE volume (also under
GROW MODE), a disk that missed the latest configuration, the platform
check and --force, global hot spares, mdmon for --add, "missing"
refused, TRIM, the repair of Legacy records, and the md write-intent
bitmap of a volume: where it lies, the md fix it needs
from Linux 6.18, and what to do after an mdadm without bitmap support
ran the volume.  Describe the --update-subarray values that set the
cache policy of an AMD volume and bitmap/no-bitmap of AMD volumes, and
--add-spare/--remove on an AMD volume (its dedicated hot spares), and
that an AMD volume name has at most 31 characters.  mdadm.conf(5)
lists amd with the known metadata types (AUTO, POLICY metadata=).

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
---
 mdadm.8.in      | 141 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 mdadm.conf.5.in |   5 +-
 2 files changed, 139 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/mdadm.8.in b/mdadm.8.in
index 168e21f2..cab702e1 100644
--- a/mdadm.8.in
+++ b/mdadm.8.in
@@ -366,6 +366,107 @@ which is managed in a similar manner to DDF, and is supported by an
 option-rom on some platforms:
 .IP
 .B https://docs.graidtech.com/vroc/
+.IP amd
+Use the AMD RAIDXpert2 ("RAIDCore") metadata of the RAID in AMD chipsets,
+as the platform firmware in RAID mode and the AMD Windows driver keep it.
+This creates a
+.B CONTAINER
+which is managed in a similar manner to IMSM.  Linear (AMD "Volume"),
+RAID0, RAID1, RAID10 and RAID5 arrays are supported; an AMD RAIDABLE
+volume ("RAIDAble" in the firmware menu: one disk kept for a future
+mirror) runs as a RAID1 of one member, and
+.B "\-\-level=1 \-\-raid\-devices=1 \-\-force"
+creates one, as the firmware menu does: on a whole disk that no volume
+uses, without
+.BR \-\-size ;
+.B "\-\-grow \-\-raid\-devices=2"
+of it makes it a RAID1 of two onto a spare of the container, as the AMD
+migration does.
+A Volume runs as an md linear array: it needs the md-linear module, which
+Linux 6.8 to 6.13 do not have and some distribution kernels do not build.
+.IP
+A RAID0, Volume or RAIDABLE array is started with a disk that missed the
+latest configuration update (after a power failure while it was being
+written, for example) if the disk's own configuration lists the same array
+in the same place: such an array cannot have been written without that
+disk.  A RAID1, RAID10 or RAID5 array treats such a disk as out of date:
+it is started without it only with
+.BR \-\-run ,
+and the disk is then rebuilt.
+.IP
+Disks are taken only behind controllers the firmware uses for
+RAID (EFI variable
+.BR AMD_RAID ),
+unless
+.B \-\-force
+is given: the firmware and Windows would not see the others.
+Disks must have 512-byte logical sectors (512n or 512e): AMD RAID has no
+format for 4Kn disks.
+.IP
+A disk added to the container with
+.B \-\-add
+becomes an AMD global hot spare, as with the firmware menu's Assign Global
+Hot Spare.  mdmon records the new disk, so it must be running for the
+container: it is started with a RAID1, RAID10, RAID5 or RAIDABLE volume,
+not with RAID0 and Volume ones.
+A volume is created on all its disks:
+.B missing
+is refused, as with IMSM.
+As with the firmware menu and the AMD Windows tools, a volume has 8 disks
+at most, and a system 12 arrays, each Legacy (non-RAID) disk counting as
+one;
+.B \-\-force
+goes past either limit.  The firmware and the Windows driver took
+volumes of up to 12 disks created so, but their tools do not create
+them, and the firmware menu creates no array past the 12.
+A volume created in the container takes global hot spares as
+members too (when no disks are named, only after the disks that are not
+spares), and they are spares no more; a dedicated hot spare of another
+volume is not taken.
+.IP
+TRIM reaches a RAID1 or RAID10 volume only while every member reads
+zeroes after it, and never a RAID5 volume, as with the AMD driver: mdmon
+sets the volume's
+.B queue/discard_max_bytes
+to 0 otherwise (the kernel log says so), and back when a new member
+allows TRIM again; from Linux 6.17 (and 6.12.42) the kernel does not take
+it back, and the volume has no TRIM until it is started again.
+A Legacy (non-RAID) record that the firmware menu broke, leaving that
+disk unbootable, is repaired with the next configuration mdadm or mdmon
+writes.
+.IP
+RAID1, RAID10 and RAID5 volumes can have an md write-intent bitmap, kept
+in the service area AMD leaves free on each member: give
+.B \-\-bitmap=internal
+to
+.BR \-\-create ,
+or
+.B \-\-update=bitmap
+(and
+.BR no\-bitmap )
+with
+.B \-\-update\-subarray
+for a stopped volume.  It is off by default.  All members of the volume
+must start at one LBA: md keeps the bits at one offset on every member.
+A volume placed after another volume on only some of its disks starts at
+different LBAs and cannot have one; create it on disks that hold the same
+volumes.  A spare that replaces a member is put at that LBA; if it has
+no room there, md drops the bitmap for good (the kernel log says so) and
+the volume goes on without it: its members no longer start at one LBA.
+Linux 6.18 and later cannot run an md bitmap for external metadata (IMSM
+too) until md is fixed ("md/md-bitmap: fix write-intent bitmap setup for
+external metadata"); without the fix mdadm says so, the volume runs
+without the bitmap, and a crash resyncs it whole.
+.IP
+A volume with an md bitmap should not be run by an mdadm that reads amd
+metadata but not its md bitmaps (an older build): such an mdadm leaves the
+bits as they are, and the next one would trust them.  If that happened,
+give
+.B \-\-update=no\-bitmap
+and then
+.B \-\-update=bitmap
+to the stopped volume before it runs again: a new bitmap has every bit
+set, so md resyncs the volume once.
 .PP
 .RE
 
@@ -1373,6 +1474,11 @@ except that it does not attempt
 first.  The device will be added as a spare even if it looks like it
 could be a recent member of the array.
 
+For an AMD RAID volume (a member array of an AMD container) the device
+becomes a dedicated hot spare of that volume: it must be in the container
+already, and only RAID1, RAID10 and RAID5 volumes take them, up to four.
+A global hot spare given so is a dedicated one from then on.
+
 .TP
 .BR \-r ", " \-\-remove
 remove listed devices.  They must not be active.  i.e. they should
@@ -1396,6 +1502,11 @@ to be removed.
 The third will remove a set as described below under
 .BR \-\-fail .
 
+For AMD RAID, removing a device from a volume (a member array) unassigns it
+as a dedicated hot spare of that volume.  Removing from the container a hot
+spare that is still connected unassigns its spare roles too; the device stays
+an initialized AMD RAID disk.
+
 .TP
 .BR \-f ", " \-\-fail
 Mark listed devices as faulty.
@@ -2269,14 +2380,19 @@ option. The supported options are
 .BR name ,
 .BR ppl ,
 .BR no\-ppl ,
-.BR bitmap
+.BR bitmap ,
+.BR no\-bitmap ,
+.BR read\-cache ,
+.BR no\-read\-cache ,
+.B write\-back
 and
-.BR no\-bitmap .
+.BR no\-write\-back .
 
 The
 .B name
 option updates the subarray name in the metadata. It cannot be longer than
-32 chars. If successes, new value will be respected after next assembly.
+32 chars (31 for AMD). If successes, new value will be respected after next
+assembly.
 
 The
 .B ppl
@@ -2290,7 +2406,17 @@ The
 and
 .B no\-bitmap
 options enable and disable write-intent bitmap in the metadata. Currently supported only for
-IMSM subarrays.
+IMSM and AMD subarrays.
+
+The
+.BR read\-cache ,
+.BR no\-read\-cache ,
+.B write\-back
+and
+.B no\-write\-back
+options set the cache policy the AMD drivers (for Windows and Linux) use
+for the volume; md does not cache.  Supported only for AMD subarrays,
+also while the array is running.
 
 .TP
 .B \-\-examine
@@ -2660,7 +2786,12 @@ container format.  The number of devices in a container can be
 increased - which affects all arrays in the container - or an array
 in a container can be converted between levels where those levels are
 supported by the container, and the conversion is on of those listed
-above.
+above.  In an AMD RAID container the only change supported is
+.B "\-\-raid\-devices=2"
+of a RAIDABLE volume, which makes it a RAID1 (see
+.B amd
+under
+.BR \-\-metadata ).
 
 .PP
 Notes:
diff --git a/mdadm.conf.5.in b/mdadm.conf.5.in
index 69434e2c..28695805 100644
--- a/mdadm.conf.5.in
+++ b/mdadm.conf.5.in
@@ -447,7 +447,8 @@ The known metadata types are
 .BR 0.90 ,
 .BR 1.x ,
 .BR ddf ,
-.BR imsm .
+.BR imsm ,
+.BR amd .
 
 .B AUTO
 should be given at most once.  Subsequent lines are silently ignored.
@@ -491,7 +492,7 @@ line and supported values are:
 any arbitrary string
 .TP
 .B metadata=
-0.9 1.x ddf or imsm
+0.9 1.x ddf imsm or amd
 .TP
 .B path=
 file glob matching anything from
